# Systemd preset overrides for the Azure Linux WSL variant.
#
# WSL manages networking, DNS resolution and the console on behalf of the
# distribution. Units that duplicate that management either fail outright or
# fight with the Windows host, and several of them fail noisily when more than
# one WSL distribution is running at the same time. See:
# https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/wsl/build-custom-distro#systemd-recommendations
#
# systemd reads preset files from all preset directories in lexicographic
# filename order and applies the first line that matches a unit, so this file
# takes precedence over both Azure Linux's 90-default.preset and systemd's own
# 90-systemd.preset. The 80- prefix matches the convention fedora-release uses
# for its variant presets (80-server.preset, 80-workstation.preset, ...).
#
# Only units with an [Install] section can be turned off from a preset.
# systemd-vconsole-setup.service, tmp.mount and the systemd-tmpfiles-* units are
# static, so azurelinux-release-wsl masks those instead. Keep the two lists in
# sync with the comment in azurelinux-release.spec.

# WSL configures the network adapters; the distribution must not.
disable systemd-networkd.service
disable systemd-networkd.socket
disable systemd-networkd-varlink.socket
disable systemd-networkd-wait-online.service

# WSL owns DNS resolution and writes /etc/resolv.conf itself.
disable systemd-resolved.service
disable systemd-resolved-monitor.socket
disable systemd-resolved-varlink.socket

# tty1 is unreachable under WSL and claiming the console races with other
# distributions running on the same host. The glob also covers the
# getty@.service template itself, which systemd's preset enables with
# DefaultInstance=tty1.
disable getty@*.service
